# In a list of doubles a NaN is the mark of a hole, so a number goes in raw only behind `x === x`; what fails leaves for the stub. The emitter held two raw stores of the library's own operations to that and none
# of the program's: a store by key, a push, an append. Under the words that make the emitters check proofs each of them is held now; as users build nothing is added.
# The lie, testing=NumbersStoredIntoListsOfDoublesAreTakenToBeNoNaN, leaves the arms' own test out: as built the element, nothing by nothing, would be gone in silence.
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all globalThis.fails = "all";
# RIGHT: true 0.5,NaN,0.5
# RIGHT-NEXT: true 1.5,0.5,NaN,0.5
# RIGHT-NEXT: true 1.5,0.5,NaN,0.5
# RIGHT-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(HELD): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime globalThis.fails = "all";
